  <h1>Explained: Making sense of the 2024–25 Union Budget</h1>
  <div><em>Indian Express | Date: Jul 24, 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>The Union Budget is India’s annual financial statement — not just numbers but a reflection of the government’s priorities and economic vision. The 2024–25 Budget was presented against a backdrop of slowing global growth, domestic unemployment concerns, and political anticipation ahead of the general elections. It aimed to strike a balance between fiscal consolidation and growth stimulation. For UPSC aspirants, this Budget provides a rich case study of India’s developmental priorities.
________________________________________
Key Highlights
1.	Fiscal Deficit Management
o	Fiscal deficit pegged at 5.1% of GDP, continuing glide path toward 4.5% by FY26.
o	Government signaling fiscal discipline amid global headwinds.
2.	Capital Expenditure Push
o	Capex allocation at ₹11.1 lakh crore, up 16% from previous year.
o	Infrastructure, logistics, and railways received major thrust.
o	Seen as a driver for crowding-in private investment.
3.	Social Welfare & Rural Focus
o	Enhanced allocations for PM-Kisan, rural housing, and health schemes.
o	New schemes for skilling and entrepreneurship for youth.
4.	Green Growth Initiatives
o	₹35,000 crore earmarked for energy transition, National Hydrogen Mission, and renewable expansion.
o	Support for EV ecosystem.
5.	Taxation
o	Continuation of simplified tax regime.
o	No major surprises, signaling stability for investors.
________________________________________
Challenges and Critiques
•	Revenue Mobilization: Tax buoyancy remains uncertain; GST collections volatile.
•	Employment: High capital expenditure may not generate proportionate jobs quickly.
•	Rural Distress: Despite allocations, concerns remain over agriculture stagnation.
•	Implementation Bottlenecks: India often struggles to convert budget allocations into timely execution.
________________________________________
Relevance for UPSC
•	GS-III (Economy):
o	Budgeting process, fiscal consolidation, capital expenditure.
o	Inclusive growth vs fiscal discipline.
•	Prelims:
o	Figures (Fiscal deficit, Capex).
o	Schemes: PM-Kisan, National Hydrogen Mission.
•	Essay:
o	“Budget as a tool for inclusive development.”
o	“Balancing fiscal prudence and welfare needs.”
•	GS-II: Governance and policy priorities reflected in budget choices.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Focus on Employment-Intensive Sectors: Labor-intensive manufacturing and services must complement infrastructure-heavy growth.<br/>2.	Enhance Revenue Collection: Strengthen GST compliance and widen direct tax base.<br/>3.	Inclusive Growth: Ensure welfare schemes reach vulnerable groups; monitor leakages.<br/>4.	Green Economy: Leverage India’s G20 leadership in climate finance to accelerate clean energy transition.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>The 2024–25 Budget reflects continuity with reform: discipline in fiscal numbers, boldness in capital investment, and sensitivity to welfare needs. Yet, India’s developmental challenge lies not just in allocations but in execution capacity. For aspirants, the Budget encapsulates the eternal governance dilemma: How to balance growth, equity, and sustainability within fiscal constraints.</p>
